==English==

===Etymology===
{{rfscript|Gothic}}
1580, from {{etyl|la}} {{term|Teutonicus|Teutōnicus||lang=la}}, from {{term|Teutones|Teutōnes|lang=la}}, {{term|Teutoni|Teutōnī|the Teutons|tr=name of a Germanic tribe that inhabited coastal Germany and devastated Gaul between 113-101 B.C.|lang=la}}, of {{etyl|gem}} origin, from {{proto|Germanic|þiudanas|ruler, leader of the people|lang=en}} from {{proto|Germanic|þiudō|people|lang=en}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|teut-|people|lang=en}}. Cognate with {{got}} {{term|𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰𐌽𐍃||tr=þiudans|king|lang=got}}, {{non}} {{term|þjóðann|þjōðann|prince, king|lang=non}}, {{osx}} {{term|þiudan||lord of the people, ruler|lang=osx}}, {{ang}} {{term|þeoden|þēoden|king, lord|lang=ang}}, {{term|þeod|þēod|nation, people, country, language|lang=ang}}, {{ru}} {{term|чужой|lang=ru||stranger}}, {{term|чудно|lang=ru||strange}}, {{term|чудо|lang=ru||miracle}}. Related to {{l|en|Dutch}}.

